          "type": "output_text",
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"skram-constance-ring-41aff011684db92f\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":0,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.96,\"analysis\":\"Ring directly avows a past loving state—“Jeg elskede dig saa højt” (“I loved you so deeply”)—as an explanation or excuse for his conduct. The utterance does not itself undertake or renew a commitment. Although it is groaned in distress, nothing indicates that the words are a reflex-like affective discharge rather than an emotionally delivered avowal.\",\"ambiguity\":\"The plea may pragmatically suggest that Ring still loves Constance, and its sincerity or manipulative purpose is contestable, but these issues do not alter its predominantly truth-conditional force.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"direct\",\"description\":\"Ring directly addresses Constance with a past-tense declaration of love during their confrontation.\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"Constance accuses Ring of violating the condition on which she returned to him. He answers that he loved her deeply, using the avowal defensively to explain his hope of winning her back and his persistent pressure upon her. Constance rejects the word as disgusting in his mouth, framing the statement as morally compromised or manipulative; nevertheless, its core force remains an avowal of an emotional state rather than a commitment or reflexive discharge.\",\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“Jeg elskede dig saa højt, Constance,” stønnede han (“I loved you so deeply, Constance,” he groaned).\",\"supports\":\"The explicit first-person, past-tense declaration strongly supports truth-conditional avowal; the reporting verb characterizes emotional delivery but does not independently establish expressive/reflexive force.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Ring says this immediately after Constance accuses him of having agreed unreservedly to her condition and then behaving shamefully.\",\"supports\":\"The utterance functions as an explanation or excuse for his subsequent conduct, reinforcing its state-reporting force.\",\"confidence\":0.97},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Constance replies: “Ti stille med det Ord, det er mig vederstyggeligt i din Mund” (“Be quiet with that word; it is disgusting to me in your mouth”).\",\"supports\":\"Her rejection shows conflict over the credibility and moral acceptability of his love language, not a distinct performative undertaking in the core utterance.\",\"confidence\":0.97},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4\",\"source\":\"supplied_metadata\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The occurrence is annotated as affirmative, past tense/aspect, and a valid explicit “I love you” construction.\",\"supports\":\"Corroborates identification of the core event as an affirmative past-state love avowal.\",\"confidence\":0.99}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T/P/E adequately represents the core utterance as a strongly truth-conditional avowal delivered with distress. Its defensive, potentially manipulative use and Constance’s hostile reception belong to contextual interpretation rather than requiring another core-force category.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":\"The translation was used only as an analytical aid; the classification is grounded in the supplied source passage.\"}"
        }
